;; Docs for text/xml.bjo, read through (:doc #:file "xml.bjodoc"). The texts
;; are samizdat markup: code in prose is @code{...}, and a literal at sign is
;; @"@".

(:doc #:module
  (summary "XML trees: names, nodes, documents, and the views over them.")
  (see xml-name xml-element element-view node-case xml-document)
  (example """
    (import (text xml) (text xml read) (text xml write))

    (match (parse-xml "<feed><entry id='1'>hello</entry></feed>")
      ((Ok doc)
       (def entry (list-head (element-children (document-root doc))))
       (println (attr-ref entry (xml-name "id")))    ; (Some 1)
       (println (text-content entry))                ; hello
       (println (document-root doc)))                ; (feed (entry (@ (id "1")) "hello"))
      ((Err e) (println e)))

    (parse-xml "<feed><entry></feed>")
    ;; (Err ...), which prints as
    ;; malformed at line 1, column 16: The 'entry' start tag on line 1 position 8 does not ...
    """)
  (reference """
    XML is three modules. @code{(text xml)} is the tree: names, nodes,
    documents, and the views over them. @code{(text xml read)} reads XML, as a
    stream of signals, as a fold, as a tree of your own or as a
    @code{Document}. @code{(text xml write)} writes it.

    Only @code{System.Xml} and @code{System.Xml.Linq} are underneath:
    @code{XmlReader} reads, @code{XmlWriter} writes and escapes, and
    @code{XName} is a name.

    @strong{The tree is opaque.} A @code{Node} is an element, a text, a
    comment or a processing instruction. Nodes are built by
    @code{xml-element}, @code{xml-text}, @code{xml-comment} and
    @code{xml-pi}, and taken apart by the views, which is what keeps every
    tree, whoever built it, to four rules:

    @itemlist{
      @item{no two text children side by side: @code{xml-element} merges them}
      @item{no empty text child: @code{xml-element} drops it}
      @item{no attribute twice: @code{xml-element} raises}
      @item{no namespace declaration among the attributes: @code{xml-element}
            raises, and the reader never gives one}}

    The reader, the writer and the modules still to come use only what is
    exported here, so the views are the whole of the access there is.

    Whitespace is never dropped by a node: a tree read with the defaults has
    every character of text its document had.

    @strong{Deep trees.} Nothing here recurses per level of nesting. A tree
    can be built by hand as deep as memory allows, and a stack overflow is not
    something .NET lets a program catch, so every walk (@code{node=?},
    @code{eq-hash}, @code{text-content}, @code{descendants}, the printer and
    the writer) keeps a stack of its own.

    @strong{Printing.} @code{->str} shows a node or a document the way SXML
    writes one. A name in a namespace is written in Clark notation.

    @codeblock[#:lang "bjolang"]|{
    (p (@ (class "note")) "hi " (b "!"))
    ({http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom}entry (@ (href "#a")))
    (*COMMENT* " a comment ")
    (*PI* xml-stylesheet "href='a.css'")
    (*TOP* (*DOCTYPE* html) (html ...))
    }|

    It is for the REPL and for debugging. Nothing promises yet that it reads
    back.

    @strong{Not here yet.} Pattern matching, typed paths, decoding into
    records, HTML and asynchronous reading are later modules, over the views
    here. HTML parsing, XML Schema, XSLT, XML 1.1 and DTD validation are not
    planned.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Names
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc XmlName
  (summary "A name: a namespace URI and a local name, with no prefix.")
  (see xml-name xml-name-local xml-name-ns)
  (reference """
    A prefix belongs to the text a name was read from or is written to, not
    to the name. So writing a parsed document may choose other prefixes than
    the input had, and means the same.

    Names are interned, so @code{=} on two of them is a reference
    comparison, and @code{->str} writes Clark notation,
    @code{{http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom}entry}, or the local name alone for a
    name in no namespace.
    """))

(:doc xml-name
  (summary "A name, in a namespace or in none.")
  (arg local "The local name: an XML name with no colon in it.")
  (key #:ns "The namespace URI; \"\" is no namespace.")
  (returns "The name.")
  (raises ArgumentException "When local is not a valid local name, such as \"a:b\", \"\" or \"1a\".")
  (see xml-name-local xml-name-ns xml-namespace)
  (example """
    (xml-name "p")
    (xml-name "entry" #:ns "http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom")
    """)
  (reference """
    A bad local name raises, as an index out of range does: it is a
    programming error, not the document's.
    """))

(:doc xml-name-local
  (summary "A name's local name.")
  (arg n "The name.")
  (returns "The local name, without namespace or prefix.")
  (see xml-name-ns))

(:doc xml-name-ns
  (summary "A name's namespace URI.")
  (arg n "The name.")
  (returns "The namespace URI, or \"\" when the name is in no namespace.")
  (see xml-name-local))

(:doc xml-namespace
  (summary "The namespace of xml:lang and xml:space.")
  (see xml-name)
  (reference """
    @code{http://www.w3.org/XML/1998/namespace}. The writer gives a name in
    it the prefix @code{xml}, which is never declared.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Small types
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc XmlPos
  (summary "Where an element begins: a line and a column.")
  (field line "The line, from 1.")
  (field column "The column of the element's name, from 1: one past the <.")
  (see element-pos)
  (reference """
    As .NET reports them. Only an element carries a position; a text, a
    comment or a processing instruction has none.
    """))

(:doc XmlAttrs
  (summary "An element's attributes: names and values, in document order.")
  (see attr-ref element-attrs))

(:doc Doctype
  (summary "A document's DOCTYPE: its name and its public and system ids.")
  (field name "The name of the root element it declares.")
  (field public-id "The public id, if there is one.")
  (field system-id "The system id, if there is one.")
  (see document-doctype)
  (reference """
    It is kept so that a document can be written back with it. Nothing
    validates against it.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Nodes
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc Node
  (summary "An element, a text, a comment or a processing instruction.")
  (see xml-element xml-text xml-comment xml-pi element-view node-case node=?)
  (reference """
    The type is opaque: nodes are built by the functions of this module and
    taken apart by its views, which keep every tree to the rules in the
    module's documentation.

    @code{=} on two nodes is @code{node=?}, and @code{eq-hash} agrees with
    it, so a node is a sound @code{Map} key. @code{->str} prints it as SXML.
    """))

(:doc xml-element
  (summary "An element.")
  (arg name "The element's name.")
  (arg attrs "Its attributes, in document order.")
  (arg children "Its children, in document order.")
  (key #:pos "Where it was read from; None for an element built by hand.")
  (returns "The element, with adjacent text children merged into one and empty ones dropped.")
  (raises ArgumentException "When an attribute is given twice, or one is a namespace declaration (xmlns or xmlns:p). The writer declares namespaces itself.")
  (see xml-text element-view)
  (example """
    (xml-element (xml-name "p")
                 (list (Tuple (xml-name "class") "note"))
                 (list (xml-text "hi ") (xml-element (xml-name "b") Nil (list (xml-text "!")))))
    """))

(:doc xml-text
  (summary "A text node.")
  (arg s "The text, unescaped.")
  (returns "The node. An empty one is allowed on its own; xml-element drops it.")
  (see xml-element text-value))

(:doc xml-comment
  (summary "A comment.")
  (arg s "What is between the comment's delimiters.")
  (returns "The node.")
  (see comment-value)
  (reference """
    Not checked here: a comment containing @code{--} or ending in @code{-}
    is refused when it is written.
    """))

(:doc xml-pi
  (summary "A processing instruction.")
  (arg target "Its target, the name after the <?.")
  (arg data "Its data, the rest.")
  (returns "The node.")
  (see pi-target pi-data)
  (reference """
    Not checked here, but when it is written, as a comment is.
    """))

(:doc element?
  (summary "Whether a node is an element.")
  (arg n "The node.")
  (returns "#t for an element.")
  (see text? comment? pi? element-view))

(:doc text?
  (summary "Whether a node is a text.")
  (arg n "The node.")
  (returns "#t for a text.")
  (see element? text-value))

(:doc comment?
  (summary "Whether a node is a comment.")
  (arg n "The node.")
  (returns "#t for a comment.")
  (see element? comment-value))

(:doc pi?
  (summary "Whether a node is a processing instruction.")
  (arg n "The node.")
  (returns "#t for a processing instruction.")
  (see element? pi-target pi-data))

(:doc element-name
  (summary "An element's name.")
  (arg n "An element.")
  (returns "Its name.")
  (raises ArgumentException "When n is not an element.")
  (see element-view))

(:doc element-attrs
  (summary "An element's attributes.")
  (arg n "An element.")
  (returns "Its attributes, in document order.")
  (raises ArgumentException "When n is not an element.")
  (see attr-ref element-view))

(:doc element-children
  (summary "An element's children.")
  (arg n "An element.")
  (returns "Its children, in document order.")
  (raises ArgumentException "When n is not an element.")
  (see element-children-only element-view))

(:doc element-pos
  (summary "Where an element was read from.")
  (arg n "An element.")
  (returns "Its position, or None for an element built without one.")
  (raises ArgumentException "When n is not an element.")
  (see XmlPos))

(:doc element-view
  (summary "An element's name, attributes and children, in one call.")
  (arg n "Any node.")
  (returns "(Some (Tuple name attrs children)) for an element, None for any other node.")
  (see element? node-case)
  (example """
    (match n
      ((:view element-view (Some (Tuple name attrs kids))) (list-length kids))
      (_ 0))
    """)
  (reference """
    It allocates nothing, since @code{Option} is a struct and @code{Tuple} a
    value tuple, which makes it the view a pattern can be compiled into.
    """))

(:doc text-value
  (summary "A text node's text.")
  (arg n "A text.")
  (returns "The text.")
  (raises ArgumentException "When n is not a text.")
  (see text-content))

(:doc comment-value
  (summary "A comment's text.")
  (arg n "A comment.")
  (returns "What is between its delimiters.")
  (raises ArgumentException "When n is not a comment."))

(:doc pi-target
  (summary "A processing instruction's target.")
  (arg n "A processing instruction.")
  (returns "Its target.")
  (raises ArgumentException "When n is not a processing instruction.")
  (see pi-data))

(:doc pi-data
  (summary "A processing instruction's data.")
  (arg n "A processing instruction.")
  (returns "Its data.")
  (raises ArgumentException "When n is not a processing instruction.")
  (see pi-target))

(:doc node-case
  (summary "Calls the handler for the kind of node it is given.")
  (arg n "The node.")
  (key #:element "Called with an element's name, attributes, children and position.")
  (key #:text "Called with a text's string.")
  (key #:comment "Called with a comment's string.")
  (key #:pi "Called with a processing instruction's target and data.")
  (key #:else "Called with the node itself when its kind has no handler.")
  (returns "What the handler answers.")
  (raises ArgumentException "When the node's kind has no handler and there is no #:else.")
  (see element-view)
  (example """
    (node-case n
      #:element (fun (name attrs kids pos) (xml-name-local name))
      #:text (fun (s) s)
      #:else (fun (other) ""))
    """)
  (reference """
    A call that writes @code{#:else} keeps working if a kind of node is ever
    added.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Helpers
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc attr-ref
  (summary "The value of an element's attribute.")
  (arg n "The node.")
  (arg name "The attribute's name.")
  (returns "Some value, or None when the element has no such attribute, or n is not an element.")
  (see element-attrs))

(:doc element-children-only
  (summary "The children of an element that are elements.")
  (arg n "The node.")
  (returns "The element children, in document order; Nil for a node that is not an element.")
  (see element-children))

(:doc text-content
  (summary "All the text below a node, joined.")
  (arg n "The node.")
  (returns "Every text below n joined in document order; a text's own value; \"\" for a comment or a processing instruction.")
  (see text-value descendants))

(:doc descendants
  (summary "Every node below a node, in document order.")
  (arg n "The node.")
  (returns "A lazy Seq of the nodes below n, not n itself. Empty for a node that is not an element.")
  (see element-children))

(:doc node=?
  (summary "Whether two nodes are the same tree.")
  (arg a "A node.")
  (arg b "Another.")
  (returns "#t when they are the same tree, positions and the order of attributes aside.")
  (see document=?)
  (reference """
    This is what @code{=} on nodes is, and @code{eq-hash} agrees with it.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Documents
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc Document
  (summary "A root element, what comes before and after it, and the doctype.")
  (see xml-document document-root)
  (reference """
    The comments and processing instructions before the root are the
    prolog, those after it the epilog. @code{=} on two documents is
    @code{document=?}, and @code{->str} prints one as SXML's
    @code{*TOP*}.
    """))

(:doc xml-document
  (summary "A document.")
  (arg root "The root element.")
  (key #:doctype "The doctype, if it has one.")
  (key #:prolog "The comments and processing instructions before the root.")
  (key #:epilog "The comments and processing instructions after it.")
  (returns "The document.")
  (raises ArgumentException "When root is not an element, or the prolog or epilog holds anything but comments and processing instructions.")
  (see document-root Doctype))

(:doc document-root
  (summary "A document's root element.")
  (arg d "The document.")
  (returns "The root, an element.")
  (see document-prolog document-epilog))

(:doc document-doctype
  (summary "A document's doctype.")
  (arg d "The document.")
  (returns "The doctype, or None when it has none.")
  (see Doctype))

(:doc document-prolog
  (summary "The comments and processing instructions before a document's root.")
  (arg d "The document.")
  (returns "Them, in document order.")
  (see document-epilog))

(:doc document-epilog
  (summary "The comments and processing instructions after a document's root.")
  (arg d "The document.")
  (returns "Them, in document order.")
  (see document-prolog))

(:doc document=?
  (summary "Whether two documents are the same.")
  (arg a "A document.")
  (arg b "Another.")
  (returns "#t when their doctypes are equal and their nodes are, as node=? compares them.")
  (see node=?)
  (reference "This is what @code{=} on documents is."))
